(
    title: "Color Bar Generator",
    kind: Test,
    developer: Some("Jerry Lawson"),
    description: Some("Not a game but a technician\'s instrument: the cartridge turns a 2600 into a TV test-pattern source — colour bars, vertical lines, solid colour fields, a circle — for adjusting a set\'s picture. Jerry Lawson, the Fairchild engineer who pioneered the ROM game cartridge itself, sold it through his startup VideoSoft at $29.95 to TV repair shops as a stand-in for pattern generators costing a thousand dollars or more; it was never aimed at players, and few were made."),
